    text  "此实例自身的外观。当 StyleSource 如此指定且项目中确实存在样式表时，项目样式表\n优先；项目中不存在样式表时，此属性就是实际生效的外观——这也是它\n保持可编辑、而不是被枚举加以门控的原因。"
    state machine
}

DreamListViewBase {
    at    Source/DreamGUIControls/Public/Controls/DreamListView.h#DreamListViewBase
    src   "Everything a list and a tree have in common, which is nearly all of it.\n\nA tree IS a list that indents, and this class is written to be exactly that: the viewport, the\nbar, the scrolled column, the row template, the row POOL, the row colours and the one selected\nindex live here, and each concrete control adds only what makes it itself -- its typed style,\nand (for the tree) a depth per item, a per-row indent and a twisty. FDreamTreeViewStyle is built\nthe same way round, carrying a whole FDreamListStyle rather than restating its fields.\n\nThis class is abstract and holds NO style property. That is deliberate, and it is the reason\nthere is a base class at all rather than UDreamTreeView deriving from UDreamListView: the\nfamily's contract is that a control's look is ONE decision (see UDreamUIControl), and a tree\ninheriting a spare FDreamListStyle it never resolves would be a second source of truth wearing\nthe same name as the real one. Each subclass declares `Style`, typed, and answers\nResolveListStyle() with the list half of it.\n\nTHE SHAPE\n---------\nA face carrying the look, a viewport clipped inside it holding the scroll behaviour, the column\nthat slides within the viewport, and a bar along the viewport's edge -- UDreamScrollBox's\narrangement, and for its reasons. Two of them are worth restating because they are not obvious:\nthe behaviour sits on the VIEWPORT and the bar is the viewport's SIBLING, because a scroll view\naccepts drags from anywhere inside its own widget, so a bar hung underneath it would scroll the\ncontent every time the handle was grabbed; and the column's height is the control's statement of\nhow far there is to scroll, never layout output.\n\nROWS ARE PLACED, NOT ARRANGED\n-----------------------------\nThe column holds NO layout container and every row states its own rect: top-anchored, stretched\nacross, and offset by its index times the row pitch. Three things follow, and all three matter:\n\n - a row's height is the style's RowHeight, full stop. The previous shape put the rows in a\n   vertical box on equal-weight Fill slots and then authored the COLUMN's height to exactly\n   rows*RowHeight + gaps so the box would divide it back out -- an equation solved in two places\n   that had to agree, and which stopped agreeing the moment anything measured a row's content;\n - placing row N costs nothing and depends on no sibling, which is what makes recycling possible\n   at all: a box arranges every child it has, so a box can never show a window onto a million;\n - and the answer is available with no layout pass at all, which is what a headless test and the\n   designer's first frame actually see.\n\nVIRTUALIZATION IS A THRESHOLD, NOT A MODE\n-----------------------------------------\nUnder VirtualizationThreshold items there is one row widget per item and GetRowWidget answers for\nevery index -- the contract this control has always had, and the one that makes it correct in a\nheadless test where no viewport has been arranged. At or above it, the pool is sized to the\nwindow plus an overscan and rows are re-bound as the view moves, which is what UMG's ListView\ndoes and the only way a hundred thousand rows is anything but a hang. GetRowWidget then answers\nfor realized rows and null for the rest, which is UMG's contract too.\n\nNAVIGATION STEPS BY ITEM, AS SListView's DOES\n--------------------------------------------\nA navigation press on a row is answered by the list (see UDreamListRowButton): along the scroll axis\nthe next or previous item -- ResolveNavigationTarget, which a tile view answers per line and per\ncolumn -- is scrolled into view and, while bSelectItemOnNavigation is on, selected, the way\nSListView::OnNavigation and NavigationSelect do it. A press with nowhere to go inside the list (past\neither end, or across a one-column list) is left to the ordinary geometric scan, which takes focus\nto whatever is beside the list -- STableViewBase::OnNavigation's answer. Arriving at a list from\noutside is that same scan, which lands on the nearest row and does not select it.\n\nMULTI SELECTION IS SListView's\n------------------------------\nIn Multi mode a click chooses the way STableRow::OnMouseButtonDown and OnMouseButtonUp do: a plain\nclick selects the row it lands on and nothing else; Ctrl adds the row or takes it away; Shift adds\nevery row from the range anchor to this one to what is already chosen, and Ctrl with Shift does the\nsame. The anchor is the row the last plain click, Ctrl click, finger tap or plain navigation step landed\non -- SListView's RangeSelectionStart -- and a Shift range leaves it where it was. A finger has no\nmodifier keys, and STableRow::OnTouchEnded gives a tap in Multi mode the one meaning it can have: it\nadds the row it lifts on and never takes one away. The keys are SListView::NavigationSelect's and\nOnKeyDown's: an arrow with Shift selects from the anchor to the row it moves to and nothing else, with\nCtrl and Shift adds that range, with Ctrl alone adds the row it moves to, and Ctrl+A selects every row.\nThe modifiers a click is read with are the clicking player's, held under their hands as the player\ncontroller has them; the Ctrl spot is shared with Cmd. Every selection is still asked of the veto\n(OnIsItemSelectableOrNavigable), and a click still selects on the release that completes it.\n\nTAB SEES ONE STOP\n-----------------\nA list is one Tab stop, as a browser's list box is: TabNavigation is Once, Tab enters at\nResolveTabEntry's row -- the selected item's, else the first item's, the last's for Shift+Tab, found\nby index, scrolled into view and built, because the rows exist only for what shows -- and the next\nTab leaves the list. The arrows move inside it. Next and Prev are never the rows' to step through\n(HandleRowNavigation leaves them to the Tab walk), and the bar is no stop at all.\n\nWHY THE PLAIN SCROLL VIEW, NOT UUIListView\n------------------------------------------\nThe recycling stack (UUIRecyclableScrollView, and UUIListView on top of it) is the right answer\nfor a data source and the wrong one for a control, for reasons that are all in those files:\n\n - its unit is a UObject* item, because IUIRecyclableScrollViewDataSource is a UObject protocol.\n   A Native.List's natural item is a line of text -- the same call FDreamDropdownStyle's options\n   make -- and feeding the recycler would mean minting a UObject per label;\n - a cell only exists after Start(), which needs a live world, a registered tree AND an arranged\n   viewport: InitializeOnDataSource sizes the cell pool from the content parent's local-space\n   extents. A control has to be correct the instant its properties are set;\n - and UUIListView::Awake hands its data-source seat to whoever claimed it first, which is how an\n   `each` block's adapter gets in. A control hosting one would be a third party to that seat.\n\nSo the rows are built here, from a template, into a column inside a plain UUIScrollView -- which\nis what UDreamDropdown's option list does, because it is the same problem.\n\nHOW THIS RELATES TO `each`\n--------------------------\nThey are the two halves of the same need and they do not compete:\n\n  `each Track in Tracks { ... }` is the LANGUAGE route. It compiles a repeated subtree into the\n  generated class, feeds a host recyclable view through UDreamUIEachAdapter, and binds each row's\n  fields to the item's members (`Text <- Track.Title`). You get arbitrary row shape and per-field\n  bindings, and you pay for it with a .dui class to live in and a row you have to draw yourself.\n\n  `Native.List` is the CONTROL route. One tag, two properties, and you have a list that looks\n  like every other list in the project because its rows come from the style sheet.\n\nReach for `each` when the ROW is the interesting part. Reach for Native.List when the LIST is."
    text  "列表和树共有的全部内容，而这几乎是它们的全部。\n\n树就是一种会缩进的列表，而这个类正是照此编写的：视口、\n滚动条、滚动列、行模板、行池、行颜色和唯一选中\n索引都在这里，每个具体控件只添加使其成为自身的东西——它的类型化样式，\n以及（对于树）每项的深度、每行缩进和一个展开箭头。FDreamTreeViewStyle 也按\n同样的方式构建，携带整个 FDreamListStyle，而不是重新声明其字段。\n\n此类是抽象类，且不包含任何样式属性。这是有意为之，也正是\n之所以存在基类、而不是让 UDreamTreeView 派生自 UDreamListView 的原因：该\n家族的约定是，控件的外观是唯一一项决策（参见 UDreamUIControl），而一棵树\n若继承一个它从不解析的闲置 FDreamListStyle，就会成为第二个事实来源，\n却与真正的那个同名。每个子类都声明类型化的 `Style`，并\n用其中的列表部分来回答 ResolveListStyle()。\n\n结构\n---------\n一个承载外观的表面，一个裁剪在其内部并承载滚动行为的视口，一个\n在视口内滑动的列，以及一条沿视口边缘的滚动条——UDreamScrollBox 的\n布局，原因也相同。其中两点值得重申，因为它们并不显而易见：\n滚动行为位于视口上，而滚动条是视口的同级，因为滚动视图\n会接受其自身控件内任意位置的拖动，所以挂在它下面的滚动条会在每次抓住滑块时滚动\n内容；列的高度是控件对可滚动距离的声明，\n绝不是布局输出。\n\n行是放置的，不是排列的\n-----------------------------\n列不包含任何布局容器，每一行都声明自己的矩形：顶部锚定、横向\n拉伸，并按索引乘以行距进行偏移。由此得出三点，且三点都很重要：\n\n - 一行的高度就是样式的 RowHeight，没有别的。之前的结构把行放进一个\n   垂直盒中、使用等权重的 Fill 槽位，然后把列的高度精确设定为\n   rows*RowHeight + gaps，以便盒再把它分配回去——这是一个在两个地方求解\n   且必须一致的方程，而一旦有任何东西测量了行的内容，它就不再一致；\n - 放置第 N 行的成本为零，且不依赖任何同级项，这正是回收之所以可能\n   的原因：盒会排列它拥有的每个子项，因此盒永远无法显示百万行中的窗口；\n - 并且答案无需任何布局过程即可获得，这正是无头测试和\n   设计师的第一帧实际看到的东西。\n\n虚拟化是阈值，不是模式\n-----------------------------------------\n当项数低于 VirtualizationThreshold 时，每个项对应一个行控件，且 GetRowWidget 会对\n每个索引作出回答——这是此控件一直以来的约定，也是让它在\n未安排任何视口的无头测试中保持正确的约定。达到或超过该阈值时，池的大小设为\n窗口加上一个 overscan，并且行会随着视图移动而重新绑定，这正是 UMG 的 ListView\n所做的，也是让十万行不至于卡死的唯一方式。此时 GetRowWidget 对\n已实现的行作出回答，对其余行返回 null，这也是 UMG 的约定。\n\n导航按项步进，与 SListView 的做法相同\n--------------------------------------------\n在行上按下导航键时，由列表来回答（参见 UDreamListRowButton）：沿滚动轴，\n下一个或上一个项——ResolveNavigationTarget，平铺视图会按行和按\n列回答——会被滚动到视野中，并且在 bSelectItemOnNavigation 开启时被选中，方式与\nSListView::OnNavigation 和 NavigationSelect 相同。若在列表内部无处可去（越过\n任一端，或横穿单列列表），则交给普通的几何扫描，它会把焦点\n移到列表旁边的任意对象——这是 STableViewBase::OnNavigation 的回答。从外部\n到达列表也是同一次扫描，它会落在最近的行上，但不选中它。\n\n多选遵循 SListView\n------------------------------\n在 Multi 模式下，单击的选择方式与 STableRow::OnMouseButtonDown 和 OnMouseButtonUp 相同：普通\n单击会选中它落到的行，除此之外不选别的；Ctrl 会添加该行或将其移除；Shift 会把\n从范围锚点到当前行的所有行添加到已选内容中，Ctrl+Shift 也是如此。\n锚点是上一次普通单击、Ctrl 单击、手指点按或普通导航步进落到的行\n——即 SListView 的 RangeSelectionStart——而 Shift 范围会把它留在原处。手指没有\n修饰键，STableRow::OnTouchEnded 让 Multi 模式下的点按具有它唯一能有的含义：它\n添加它抬起时所在的行，且绝不移除任何行。按键行为来自 SListView::NavigationSelect 和\nOnKeyDown：带 Shift 的方向键会选中从锚点到其移动到的行，除此之外不选别的；带\nCtrl 和 Shift 会添加该范围；仅带 Ctrl 会添加其移动到的行；Ctrl+A 会选中所有行。\n读取单击所用的修饰键来自执行单击的玩家，即其玩家\n控制器所保持的按键状态；Ctrl 的位置与 Cmd 共享。每次选择仍会经过否决\n（OnIsItemSelectableOrNavigable），而单击仍在完成它的释放时进行选择。\n\nTab 只看到一个停靠点\n-----------------\n列表是一个 Tab 停靠点，就像浏览器的列表框一样：TabNavigation 为 Once，Tab 从\nResolveTabEntry 的行进入——选中项的行，否则是第一项的行，Shift+Tab 则是最后一项的行，按\n索引找到、滚动到视野中并构建，因为行只为显示出来的内容而存在——而下一次\nTab 会离开列表。方向键在其内部移动。Next 和 Prev 从来不由行来步进\n（HandleRowNavigation 将它们留给 Tab 遍历），而滚动条根本不是停靠点。\n\n为什么使用普通滚动视图，而不是 UUIListView\n------------------------------------------\n回收栈（UUIRecyclableScrollView，以及其上的 UUIListView）对于数据源是正确答案，\n对于控件则是错误答案，原因都在那些文件中：\n\n - 它的单位是 UObject* 项，因为 IUIRecyclableScrollViewDataSource 是一个 UObject 协议。\n   Native.List 的自然项是一行文本——FDreamDropdownStyle 的选项也做同样的\n   选择——而喂给回收器意味着为每个标签创建一个 UObject；\n - 单元格只有在 Start() 之后才存在，而 Start() 需要运行中的世界、已注册的树以及已排布的\n   视口：InitializeOnDataSource 根据内容父项的局部空间\n   范围确定单元格池的大小。控件必须在设置其属性的那一刻就是正确的；\n - 并且 UUIListView::Awake 会将其数据源席位交给最先认领它的人，`each`\n   块的适配器就是这样进入的。承载这种视图的控件会成为该席位的第三方。\n\n因此这里的行是从模板构建到普通 UUIScrollView 内的列中的——这\n正是 UDreamDropdown 的选项列表所做的，因为这是同一个问题。\n\n这与 `each` 的关系\n--------------------------\n它们是同一需求的两半，并不相互竞争：\n\n  `each Track in Tracks { ... }` 是语言路线。它把重复的子树编译进\n  生成的类，通过 UDreamUIEachAdapter 向宿主可回收视图提供数据，并将每一行的\n  字段绑定到项的成员（`Text <- Track.Title`）。你可以获得任意的行形状和逐字段\n  绑定，代价是需要一个 .dui 类来安身，并且行必须自己绘制。\n\n  `Native.List` 是控件路线。一个标签、两个属性，你就得到一个看起来\n  像项目中其他所有列表一样的列表，因为它的行来自样式表。\n\n当行本身是有趣的部分时，选择 `each`。当列表本身是有趣的部分时，选择 Native.List。"
    state machine
}

DreamListViewBase:ActiveDragOperation {
